import FormalConjectures.GreensOpenProblems.«14»

/-!
# A reduction of W(3,13) by two separated blue runs

On a board avoiding red triples and blue thirteen-term progressions, every
69-point window contains five consecutive blues. The corresponding sharp
bounds for six and seven consecutive blues are 91 and 113. Each certificate
adds the consecutive intervals to the thirteen-term progressions required
to meet the red set. Its branches choose whether a point is red. A red
pair eliminates all completions of a triple, and a window with only one
remaining point forces it red.

A seven-blue run in the central 113 positions belongs to an interior gap
between reds. Reflect to put the midpoint of that gap on the left. A
second interior gap to its right contains at least five blues. The two
open cases distinguish a shared red endpoint from four distinct endpoints.

The search verifier adapts the bit-mask method of MrSeizy, Vincent T.
Mossman and PanPapag (PRs 1192, 1879, 2630 and 3245), including the
chunked central-gap verifier of Green14.W_3_13.R1.H3. All three search
trees, the sharpness witnesses and the two-gap argument are new here.
NickLeko's R1 supplies the 159-point coloring and the extremal-number
argument, building on Halldon and Dominic Dabish. Those portions are
adapted under Apache License 2.0, copyright 2026 The Formal Conjectures
Authors. The original numerical value is due to Ahmed, Kullmann and Snevily.
-/

